questions.detailed:
**Push most testing down into each domain, and add contract and composition tests for the seams.** End-to-end tests alone are slow and cannot tell which team broke what.

{{diagram:pyramid}}

## Layers

| Layer | Scope | Owner |
|---|---|---|
| Unit | Functions, hooks, logic | Domain team |
| Component | A domain's components in isolation (with mocked APIs) | Domain team |
| Contract | Host and remote agree on props, events, URLs and API shapes | Both sides |
| Integration | Shell plus real or staged remotes composed together | Platform with domains |
| End-to-end | A few critical journeys, for example browse to quote | Shared, kept small |

## Contract tests

The remote's exposed component and its events are the contract. A consumer-driven approach has the host (and neighbours) record what they rely on, and the remote's pipeline verifies it, so a breaking change fails before deploy.

```ts
// a simple contract check on an exposed module's behaviour
import { render, screen } from '@testing-library/react';
import VehiclePage from 'vehicle/VehiclePage';

test('renders with required props and emits selection event', async () => {
  const handler = vi.fn();
  window.addEventListener('vehicle:selected', handler);
  render(<VehiclePage vehicleId="v-123" />);
  (await screen.findByRole('button', { name: /select/i })).click();
  expect(handler).toHaveBeenCalled();
});
```

## Other checks

- Run each remote **standalone** with a mocked shell context.
- **Accessibility and visual regression** on shared components and key pages.
- **Failure tests:** force a remote to fail and assert the fallback appears.
- **Synthetic monitoring** in production for key journeys.

## Common mistakes

Relying on one big E2E suite; no test of the failure path; testing the remote only standalone and never composed with the real shell and shared versions.
